{
  Interface constants used to describe HUB75 panel connection and configuration
}

CON ' ---- Hardware Interface Constants ----
    {Spin2_Doc_CON}

' ---- (1) HUB75 Pin Group: where the P2 EVAL HUB75 Adapter is plugged in ----
'  A HUB75 adapter is a 16-pin board; it mounts on one 16-pin group. The pin range
'  is carried in each name, so no math is needed to know which pins are involved.
'  PIN_GROUP_P48_P63 is NOT provided: pins 48-63 overlap the P2's predefined (reserved) pins.
'  Allocation: quantity. The value is the group's base pin (0, 16, 32), used in pin arithmetic.
    PINS_PER_GROUP = 16                                         ' pins in one HUB75 pin group
    #0[PINS_PER_GROUP], PIN_GROUP_P0_P15, PIN_GROUP_P16_P31, PIN_GROUP_P32_P47

'  (2) how many HUB75 boards are you connecting?
'  HUB75 adapter identifiers (each step is HUB75_ADAPTER_STEP apart; the values are not meant to be written):
'   HUB75_ADAPTER_1 (10) - first  HUB75 adapter card
'   HUB75_ADAPTER_2 (20) - second HUB75 adapter card
'   HUB75_ADAPTER_3 (30) - third  HUB75 adapter card
'  HUB75_ADAPTER_BASE is the first identifier (the same value as HUB75_ADAPTER_1).
'  Allocation: distinct identity. The identifiers are 10, 20 and 30 so that an adapter identifier is
'  never mistaken for a chain index (0 .. 2); indexForHub75ChainId() converts one to the other.
    HUB75_ADAPTER_STEP = 10
    #HUB75_ADAPTER_STEP[HUB75_ADAPTER_STEP], HUB75_ADAPTER_1, HUB75_ADAPTER_2, HUB75_ADAPTER_3
    HUB75_ADAPTER_BASE = HUB75_ADAPTER_1

'  (3) rotation values
'  One set of values for the display's mounting (DISPn_ROTATION), for a panel's own turn (the
'  driver derives it from the panel's arrow word) and for content rotation (placeBMP).
'  Clockwise degrees:
'   ROT_UNKNOWN - rotation not yet specified
'   ROT_0       - no rotation (0 degrees)
'   ROT_90      - rotate content 90 degrees clockwise
'   ROT_180     - rotate content 180 degrees
'   ROT_270     - rotate content 270 degrees clockwise
'  Allocation: distinct identity. The values start at $20, clear of the adapter identifiers
'  (10 .. 30) and of the chain indexes (0 .. 2), so a rotation is never mistaken for either.
    #$20, ROT_UNKNOWN, ROT_0, ROT_90, ROT_180, ROT_270
    ' aliases of the values above, kept for v3 configs (prefer ROT_0, ROT_270 and ROT_90):
    ROT_NONE = ROT_0                                            ' alias kept for v3 configs; prefer ROT_0
    ROT_LEFT_90 = ROT_270                                       ' alias kept for v3 configs; prefer ROT_270
    ROT_RIGHT_90 = ROT_90                                       ' alias kept for v3 configs; prefer ROT_90
'  For DISPn_ROTATION the value is physical, how the whole display is mounted:
'   ROT_RIGHT_90 = hung turned 90 degrees clockwise, ROT_LEFT_90 = counter-clockwise;
'   the driver turns the content so it reads upright as mounted.

'  (4) The Driver Chip on your panels
' Driver Pre-defined flag combinations (use these unless your hardware is not in this list)
'  Panel driver chip selections:
'   CHIP_UNKNOWN                 - driver chip not yet specified
'   CHIP_MANUAL_SPEC             - manual specification base (OR with config flags below)
'   CHIP_FM6126A                 - FM6126A driver chip
'   CHIP_FM6124                  - FM6124 driver chip
'   CHIP_UNK_LAT_END_ENCL        - unknown chip, latch-at-end-enclosed style
'   CHIP_ICN2037                 - ICN2037 driver chip
'   CHIP_ICN2038S                - ICN2038S driver chip
'   CHIP_UNK_LAT_END_ENCL_SLO_CLK - unknown chip, latch-at-end-enclosed, slow clock
'   CHIP_MBI5124GP               - MBI5124GP driver chip
'   CHIP_GS6238S                 - GS6238S driver chip
'   CHIP_DP5125D                 - DP5125D driver chip
'  Allocation: arbitrary. The chip selections start at 0 (CHIP_UNKNOWN, unset) and occupy the low
'  byte (CHIP_TYPE_MASK); the config flags below start at $100, so a chip selection and the flags
'  OR'd onto CHIP_MANUAL_SPEC never share bits.
    #0, CHIP_UNKNOWN, CHIP_MANUAL_SPEC, CHIP_FM6126A, CHIP_FM6124, CHIP_UNK_LAT_END_ENCL, CHIP_ICN2037, ...
        CHIP_ICN2038S, CHIP_UNK_LAT_END_ENCL_SLO_CLK, CHIP_MBI5124GP, CHIP_GS6238S, CHIP_DP5125D

' Normal use:
'   PANEL_DRIVER_CHIP = CHIP_FM6126A
'
' Manual Use when your hardware is not listed:
'   PANEL_DRIVER_CHIP = CHIP_MANUAL_SPEC | {flag1} | {flag2} | etc..

' NOTE:
'   PANEL_DRIVER_CHIP = CHIP_FM6126A    ' which is: CHIP_MANUAL_SPEC | LAT_STYLE_OFFSET | LAT_POSN_OVERLAP | INIT_PANEL_REQUIRED
'   PANEL_DRIVER_CHIP = CHIP_FM6124     ' which is: CHIP_MANUAL_SPEC
'   PANEL_DRIVER_CHIP = CHIP_GS6238S    ' which is: CHIP_MANUAL_SPEC | LAT_STYLE_OFFSET | LAT_POSN_OVERLAP | GB_SWAP
'   PANEL_DRIVER_CHIP = CHIP_ICN2037    ' which is: CHIP_MANUAL_SPEC | RB_SWAP
'   PANEL_DRIVER_CHIP = CHIP_ICN2038S   ' which is: CHIP_MANUAL_SPEC | SCAN_4
'   PANEL_DRIVER_CHIP = CHIP_MBI5124GP  ' which is: CHIP_MANUAL_SPEC | CHIP_UNK_LAT_END_ENCL | SCAN_4 | INIT_PANEL_REQUIRED
'   PANEL_DRIVER_CHIP = CHIP_DP5125D    ' which is: CHIP_MANUAL_SPEC | LAT_STYLE_OFFSET | LAT_POSN_OVERLAP | SCAN_4

' Driver Manual Config Flags:
'  Bit flags OR'd onto CHIP_MANUAL_SPEC to describe non-listed hardware:
'   LAT_STYLE_OFFSET    ($100)  - latch pulse uses offset style
'   LAT_POSN_OVERLAP    ($200)  - latch position overlaps last clock
'   INIT_PANEL_REQUIRED ($400)  - panel requires power-on init sequence
'   RB_SWAP             ($1000) - swap red and blue color channels
'   SCAN_4              ($2000) - 1/4 scan panel addressing
'   GB_SWAP             ($4000) - swap green and blue color channels
'  Allocation: bit field. Each flag is one bit above the chip byte, so flags combine with |.
    #$100, LAT_STYLE_OFFSET, #$200, LAT_POSN_OVERLAP, #$400, INIT_PANEL_REQUIRED, #$1000, RB_SWAP, #$2000, SCAN_4, #$4000, GB_SWAP

'  (5) Describe the overall layout of your hardware panel(s):
' SUPPORTED address widths:  A-B-C, or A-B-C-D, or A-B-C-D-E
'  Row-address line widths:
'   ADDR_UNKNOWN - address width not yet specified
'   ADDR_ABC     - 3 address lines (A-B-C)
'   ADDR_ABCD    - 4 address lines (A-B-C-D)
'   ADDR_ABCDE   - 5 address lines (A-B-C-D-E)
'  Allocation: quantity. The value is the number of address lines (ADDR_ABC = 3), used in arithmetic;
'  ADDR_UNKNOWN (2) is below the supported range.
    #2, ADDR_UNKNOWN, ADDR_ABC, ADDR_ABCD, ADDR_ABCDE

'  (6) Describe the color depth you'd like to display:
' SUPPORTED depths:  3-8 bits
'  Color depth per channel:
'   DEPTH_3BIT - 3 bits per color channel
'   DEPTH_4BIT - 4 bits per color channel
'   DEPTH_5BIT - 5 bits per color channel
'   DEPTH_6BIT - 6 bits per color channel
'   DEPTH_7BIT - 7 bits per color channel
'   DEPTH_8BIT - 8 bits per color channel
'  Allocation: quantity. The value is the number of bits per channel (DEPTH_3BIT = 3), used in arithmetic.
    #3, DEPTH_3BIT, DEPTH_4BIT, DEPTH_5BIT, DEPTH_6BIT, DEPTH_7BIT, DEPTH_8BIT

'  (7) Cube faces (a display with DISPn_SHAPE = SHAPE_CUBE):
'
'  Six square panels, wired as one of the 11 cube nets, form a cube. DISPn_CUBE_TOP
'  and DISPn_CUBE_FRONT name the cable positions of the top face and the front face;
'  the front face shares an edge with the top face. At startup the driver folds the
'  net and derives the other four faces from those two.
'  Cube face identifiers:
'   FACE_TOP    - the face at DISPn_CUBE_TOP
'   FACE_BOTTOM - the face opposite the top
'   FACE_FRONT  - the face at DISPn_CUBE_FRONT
'   FACE_BACK   - the face opposite the front
'   FACE_LEFT   - the face on the viewer's left, standing at the front
'   FACE_RIGHT  - the face on the viewer's right, standing at the front
'
'  Face coordinates (face, row, column): every face is seen from outside the cube,
'  row 0 is the face's up edge and column 0 its left edge.
'  STABLE -- display code and the cube layer depend on these up directions:
'   FRONT, BACK, LEFT, RIGHT - up points toward TOP
'   TOP                      - up points toward BACK, so that standing at FRONT, text on TOP reads correctly
'   BOTTOM                   - up points toward FRONT
'  The 12 edges: FRONT-TOP, FRONT-RIGHT, FRONT-BOTTOM, FRONT-LEFT, BACK-TOP, BACK-LEFT,
'  BACK-BOTTOM, BACK-RIGHT, LEFT-TOP, LEFT-BOTTOM, RIGHT-TOP and RIGHT-BOTTOM.
'  Allocation: distinct identity. The face identifiers start at $50, apart from the rotations ($20 ..)
'  and the shapes ($60 ..), so a face is never mistaken for either.
#$50, FACE_TOP, FACE_BOTTOM, FACE_FRONT, FACE_BACK, FACE_LEFT, FACE_RIGHT

'  (8) Wiring words: the words of the per-panel sentences DISPn_C0 .. DISPn_C15
'
'  Each sentence describes the panel at one cable position. Each word is a single
'  bit, and each kind of word has its own bit range, so a sentence that holds two
'  words of the same kind can always be detected:
'   C0 .. C15   - bits 0-15:  neighbor, the cable position of the panel this one sits next to
'   ABOVE       - bit 16:     direction, this panel sits above its neighbor
'   BELOW       - bit 17:     direction, this panel sits below its neighbor
'   LEFT_OF     - bit 18:     direction, this panel sits left of its neighbor
'   RIGHT_OF    - bit 19:     direction, this panel sits right of its neighbor
'   ARROW_UP    - bit 20:     arrow, this panel's identify arrow points up
'   ARROW_DOWN  - bit 21:     arrow, this panel's identify arrow points down (mounted at 180 degrees)
'   ARROW_LEFT  - bit 22:     arrow, this panel's identify arrow points left
'   ARROW_RIGHT - bit 23:     arrow, this panel's identify arrow points right
'   FIRST_PANEL - bit 24:     this is C0, the panel the adapter plugs into
'   NO_PANEL    - bit 25:     no panel at this cable position
'
'  Allocation: bit field. See the table above; each kind of word has its own bits.
'  Join the words with | only, never +: C2 + C2 silently becomes C3.
'  The C0 .. C15 words also name a cable position in DISPn_CUBE_TOP and DISPn_CUBE_FRONT.

    ' lowest bit of each kind of word
    NEIGHBOR_LSB        = 0                                 ' bit of C0
    DIRECTION_LSB       = 16                                ' bit of ABOVE
    ARROW_LSB           = 20                                ' bit of ARROW_UP
    FIRST_PANEL_LSB     = 24                                ' bit of FIRST_PANEL
    NO_PANEL_LSB        = 25                                ' bit of NO_PANEL

    ' neighbor words: the neighbor's cable position
    C0                  = 1 << NEIGHBOR_LSB
    C1                  = C0 << 1
    C2                  = C1 << 1
    C3                  = C2 << 1
    C4                  = C3 << 1
    C5                  = C4 << 1
    C6                  = C5 << 1
    C7                  = C6 << 1
    C8                  = C7 << 1
    C9                  = C8 << 1
    C10                 = C9 << 1
    C11                 = C10 << 1
    C12                 = C11 << 1
    C13                 = C12 << 1
    C14                 = C13 << 1
    C15                 = C14 << 1

    ' direction words: where this panel sits relative to its neighbor
    ABOVE               = 1 << DIRECTION_LSB
    BELOW               = ABOVE << 1
    LEFT_OF             = BELOW << 1
    RIGHT_OF            = LEFT_OF << 1

    ' arrow words: the panel rotation, as the direction the identify arrow points
    ARROW_UP            = 1 << ARROW_LSB
    ARROW_DOWN          = ARROW_UP << 1
    ARROW_LEFT          = ARROW_DOWN << 1
    ARROW_RIGHT         = ARROW_LEFT << 1

    ' first-panel word: marks C0, and is used only in DISPn_C0
    FIRST_PANEL         = 1 << FIRST_PANEL_LSB

    ' no-panel word: this cable position is unused
    NO_PANEL            = 1 << NO_PANEL_LSB

    ' field masks: all the bits of one kind of word
    NEIGHBOR_MASK       = C0 | C1 | C2 | C3 | C4 | C5 | C6 | C7 | C8 | C9 | C10 | C11 | C12 | C13 | C14 | C15
    DIRECTION_MASK      = ABOVE | BELOW | LEFT_OF | RIGHT_OF
    ARROW_MASK          = ARROW_UP | ARROW_DOWN | ARROW_LEFT | ARROW_RIGHT
    FIRST_PANEL_MASK    = FIRST_PANEL
    NO_PANEL_MASK       = NO_PANEL

'  (9) Display shape (DISPn_SHAPE):
'   SHAPE_FLAT - the panels form one flat display; DISPn_ROTATION applies
'   SHAPE_CUBE - six square panels form a cube; DISPn_CUBE_TOP and DISPn_CUBE_FRONT apply
'  Allocation: distinct identity. The shapes start at $60, apart from the faces ($50 ..) and the
'  rotations ($20 ..).
    #$60, SHAPE_FLAT, SHAPE_CUBE

CON ' ---- Status Codes ----
    {Spin2_Doc_CON}

'  Status codes returned by the driver methods that perform an operation
'  (allocation: arbitrary; SUCCESS is 0 and each error is a distinct negative number):
'   SUCCESS             (0)  - the operation was done
'   E_FRAMESET_NULL     (-1) - a frame set was expected and the address given was NULL
'   E_FRAMESET_FOREIGN  (-2) - the frame set is not one of the adapter's own frame sets
'   E_BAD_ROTATION      (-3) - the content rotation is not ROT_NONE, ROT_RIGHT_90, ROT_LEFT_90 or ROT_180
'   E_BMP_NOT_24BIT     (-4) - the BMP file is not an uncompressed 24-bit image
'   E_NOT_ONE_BLOCK     (-5) - the glyph does not lie wholly inside one panel, so it was not drawn as one block
    SUCCESS             = 0
    E_FRAMESET_NULL     = -1
    E_FRAMESET_FOREIGN  = -2
    E_BAD_ROTATION      = -3
    E_BMP_NOT_24BIT     = -4
    E_NOT_ONE_BLOCK     = -5

CON ' ---- Memory Units ----
    {Spin2_Doc_CON}

'  Defined here once, because this is the lowest object that the configuration file, the buffer
'  objects and the test programs can all see (the configuration file cannot see the driver):
'   BYTES_PER_LONG - bytes in a LONG (a quantity, used in arithmetic)
    BYTES_PER_LONG      = 4

PUB null()
'' This is not a top level object
''

